 "language_probability": 0.97353196144104,
 "text": "In the Chinese novel Journey to the West, the monkey hero's surname comes from the word for macaque, with the animal part removed. Without the animal part, the second character leaves boy and infant, so his surname is Sun. In 1942, Arthur Waley's English translation left this out, and a footnote translated Sun as Monkey. Waley called the book Monkey, too. He kept 30 of the hundred chapters. In the novel, Heaven gives the monkey a post in the imperial stables. 避马温 避马温 The title is usually heard as Ward Off Horse Plague. Chinese stables kept a macaque for that job. In the oldest surviving edition, the chapter titles call him the Mind Monkey. The scholar Hu Shih introduced Waley's book to Americans as humor and satire, freed from allegorical readings. Waley's monkey travels west with Pigsy the pig, Sandy the river monster, and Tripitaka the monk. In 2021, Julia Lovell's translation kept all four names, and its hero is still Monkey.",
